WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:05.310
Watching another person cry is an uncomfortable
and intense experience, even when that person

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:06.759
is only on a screen.

00:00:06.759 --> 00:00:10.750
When done well, crying scenes can pack a powerful
and emotional punch, but there are plenty

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:13.820
of crying scenes are just plain awkward to
sit through.

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:18.840
From unintentionally hilarious to just plain
cringe-worthy, here are some of the worst.

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:21.830
Very sad spoilers ahead.

00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:22.830
The Room

00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:27.820
In the climax of Tommy Wiseau's cult classic
The Room, Johnny has just lost everything.

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:31.949
His fiancee has left him, his best friend
hates him, and his life has proven to be just

00:00:31.949 --> 00:00:33.310
a shallow mirage.

00:00:33.310 --> 00:00:37.040
In his rage and grief, he destroys the apartment
he once shared with Lisa, the love of his

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:38.040
life.

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:42.440
The problem is that, as anybody who has seen
this so-bad-it's-good film can attest, Wiseau's

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:47.480
acting prowess resembles that of an alien
asked to replicate human behavior after watching

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:49.290
two episodes of Days of Our Lives.

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:51.570
"I did not hit her, it's not true!

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:52.570
It's bulls---!

00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:53.570
I did not hit her.

00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:54.560
I did naht!

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:56.440
O hai, Mark."

00:00:56.680 --> 00:01:02.460
As Johnny flails about in a cyclone of destruction,
his bizarre, guttural wailing takes the awkwardness

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:04.830
of this scene to the next level.

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:06.320
"Ahhhh!

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:08.640
Why, Lisa, why.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:09.680
WHY?"

00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:14.280
It almost sounds like he's crying, but the
discerning eye would be hard-pressed to find

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:16.220
any real tears on Wiseau's face.

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:20.760
The scene is horrific, surreal and hard to
watch, but then again, so is the rest of The

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:22.280
Room.

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:23.780
Vampire's Kiss

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:26.740
Nobody does over-the-top quite like Nicolas
Cage.

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:31.030
From his bonkers performance in Bad Lieutenant:
Port of Call New Orleans to the magnum opus

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:33.740
that is The Wicker Man, he sets a high bar
for the overdramatic.

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:35.340
"Oh, no!

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:36.800
NOT THE BEES!

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:37.800
Not the bees!"

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:42.500
But those movies don't hold a candle to how
over the top Cage gets in Vampire's Kiss.

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:46.950
The film is full of classic bits, but if there's
a single moment that embodies the awkward

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:49.920
glory of Cage's performance, it's when he
cries.

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:54.080
His character has just tried to off himself,
but the gun he was using was loaded with blanks.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:59.000
In his anguish, he whimpers quietly before
erupting into bombastic hoots.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:00.040
"BWHOOOOO!"

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:04.581
Cage's filmography is filled with classic
amped-up emotional breakdowns like this, but

00:02:04.581 --> 00:02:07.659
Vampire's Kiss is definitely one of the most
memorable.

00:02:07.659 --> 00:02:09.940
Harry Potter and the Goblet of Fire

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:14.310
Daniel Radcliffe has turned in many memorable
performances starring in the Harry Potter

00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:19.130
series, expertly nailing moments of comedy,
tragedy, and the trials of adolescence.

00:02:19.130 --> 00:02:23.270
But when you play the same character for as
long as he has, you're bound to have a few

00:02:23.270 --> 00:02:24.920
moments that fall flat.

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:28.410
At the end of Harry Potter and the Goblet
of Fire, Harry has just witnessed the return

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:32.599
of the evil Voldemort, as well as the death
of his friend Cedric Diggory Teleporting from

00:02:32.599 --> 00:02:36.640
this nightmare back into the epic conclusion
of the Triwizard Tournament, the contrast

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:40.599
between Harry's grief and the jubilant, oblivious
crowd is chilling.

00:02:40.599 --> 00:02:46.380
It doesn't help when Jeff Rawle, playing Cedric's
father, swoops in and overacts as well.

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:48.480
"WAHHHHHHH!"

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:52.290
With two actors uncomfortably sobbing over
a corpse, the scene shifts from poignant to

00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:53.370
just plain awkward.

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:57.810
A scene like this isn't supposed to be fun
to watch, but it's also probably not supposed

00:02:57.810 --> 00:02:59.690
to be this uncomfortable.

00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:01.709
Moulin Rouge!

00:03:01.709 --> 00:03:05.810
It's a safe bet that director Baz Luhrmann
never learned the meaning of 'subtlety.'

00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:09.190
While this has often led to some of the most
sweeping, beautiful and unforgettable scenes

00:03:09.190 --> 00:03:12.230
in film history, other times… not so much.

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:14.940
"They're trying to kill you!"

00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:17.660
"Shut up!"

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:21.959
This duality is best exemplified in Moulin
Rouge!, where audiences are left squirming

00:03:21.959 --> 00:03:25.280
through some comedically over-the-top crying
from Ewan McGregor.

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:29.209
In the film's climax, McGregor's character
Christian is caressing the body of his love,

00:03:29.209 --> 00:03:31.710
Satine, who has just died in his arms.

00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:36.989
Christian begins with some soft whimpering,
but soon revs up to an almost musical crescendo.

00:03:36.989 --> 00:03:41.660
"UNGH HUH HUNH HUHHH!"

00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:44.870
Considering that the film is a musical, this
isn't entirely inappropriate.

00:03:44.870 --> 00:03:49.590
While Luhrmann includes many scenes in Moulin
Rouge! that were actually SUPPOSED to be funny,

00:03:49.590 --> 00:03:54.260
this crying scene is probably the funniest
moment in the entire film.

00:03:54.260 --> 00:03:55.569
Signs

00:03:55.569 --> 00:04:00.629
The most vulnerable scene in M. Night Shyamalan's
Signs is carried out by four very talented

00:04:00.629 --> 00:04:02.019
and capable actors.

00:04:02.019 --> 00:04:05.349
Unfortunately, with a family eating what they
believe will be their last meal before an

00:04:05.349 --> 00:04:10.310
alien invasion, we get a prime example of
there being too much talent in one room.

00:04:10.310 --> 00:04:12.110
"Stop crying!"

00:04:12.110 --> 00:04:12.600
"Graham..."

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:13.340
"Don't yell at her!"

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:17.600
"Alright…"

00:04:17.609 --> 00:04:22.759
Everyone in this scene is a wonderful actor,
but with all the weeping and Mel Gibson aggressively

00:04:22.759 --> 00:04:27.189
stabbing at his food, the scene goes past
poignant and into hilarious.

00:04:27.189 --> 00:04:32.729
It's hard not to laugh as an all-American
family breaks down over french toast and spaghetti.

00:04:32.729 --> 00:04:34.020
Rocky III

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:38.680
The Rocky franchise is a national treasure,
and Sylvester Stallone brings the iconic,

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:43.909
eponymous boxer to life with an emotional
fragility that has endeared him to audiences.

00:04:43.909 --> 00:04:47.639
That emotion is front and center when Rocky
has to say goodbye to his dying trainer.

00:04:47.639 --> 00:04:52.240
A heavyweight boxer sobbing crocodile tears
down his smashed-up face is obviously not

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:53.240
gonna be pretty.

00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:55.700
But man, is it tough to watch.

00:04:55.700 --> 00:04:58.660
[inaudible Rocky mumbling]

00:04:59.260 --> 00:05:04.979
The scene is heartbreaking, but Stallone's
overacting takes it from tragedy to unintentional

00:05:04.979 --> 00:05:05.979
comedy.

00:05:05.979 --> 00:05:09.600
We may love Rocky for how in touch he is with
his emotions, but Stallone may have done his

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:12.470
job a bit too well this time.

00:05:12.470 --> 00:05:14.189
The Spider-Man trilogy

00:05:14.189 --> 00:05:18.569
There are few cinematic wailers better (or
worse, depending on your perspective) than

00:05:18.569 --> 00:05:19.699
Tobey Maguire.

00:05:19.699 --> 00:05:24.369
In Sam Raimi's Spider-Man trilogy, Maguire
cries so much as Peter Parker that it was

00:05:24.369 --> 00:05:26.580
tough to narrow things down to just one scene.

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:29.770
When he's not fighting crime, you're bound
to find him scrunching his face up into a

00:05:29.770 --> 00:05:31.189
tiny ball of despair.

00:05:31.189 --> 00:05:35.719
That said, Peter Parker is a great character
precisely because of how human he is.

00:05:35.719 --> 00:05:39.630
There's honestly a lot to love about how strange
Maguire looks when he cries, because let's

00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:42.069
be real: we all look awful when we cry.

00:05:42.069 --> 00:05:43.529
Real tears are ugly.

00:05:43.529 --> 00:05:47.400
As uncomfortable as it may be to watch, Maguire's
waterworks in the Spider-Man movies are about

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:51.030
as real as they get, meme-worthy facial expressions
and all.

00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:52.479
Thanks for watching!

